          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What screening tool does guidance describe for anxiety, and what does the score change?",
          "answer": "Guidance on anxiety screening points to a seven-item questionnaire for generalized anxiety disorder (GAD-7). It is the tool most often used both to screen and to track severity. Source: \"Generalized anxiety disorder: screening instruments and first-line care.\"\n\nThe score is read in bands:\n- 5 or more: mild\n- 10 or more: moderate\n- 15 or more: severe\n\nA score of 10 is the threshold at which further assessment usually follows. A score alone does not make a diagnosis. It is a prompt for a conversation with a clinician.\n\nThe same guidance says the evidence is insufficient to recommend for or against screening adults aged 65 and over. The strongest statement is for adults under 65. The source notes this is a statement about the evidence, not a recommendation against screening.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not say how the score is used in treatment decisions, and did not cover the first-line care they mention in the title. If you want to know more, a clinician can explain the next steps.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
